        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{P(B\\mid A)}{P(A\\mid B)}=\\dfrac{P(B)}{P(A)}$, by expanding the two conditional probabilities and cancelling $P(A\\cap B)$.",
          "worked": "Independence gives $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)>0$, so the common factor can be cancelled.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{P(B\\mid A)}{P(A\\mid B)}&=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}\\div\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}\\\\\\frac{P(B\\mid A)}{P(A\\mid B)}&=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}\\times\\frac{P(B)}{P(A\\cap B)}\\\\\\frac{P(B\\mid A)}{P(A\\mid B)}&=\\frac{P(B)}{P(A)}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
